# T-242: shell scripts MUST stay LF regardless of a committer's local
# core.autocrlf setting — a CRLF shebang line ("#!/usr/bin/env bash\r")
# fails with "bad interpreter" on the Linux deploy runner. Mirrors the
# T-217 lesson (em-dashes broke deploy.yml on Windows) for the opposite
# direction: this repo's contributors are on Windows, its deploy targets
# are Linux.
*.sh text eol=lf

# W-164b: same class for ESM launchers with a shebang (web/scripts/run-prod-verify.mjs,
# lint-ci-gate.mjs, scripts/*.mjs): a CRLF checkout on Windows makes vite fail to strip
# "#!/usr/bin/env node
" and every test importing the module dies with
# "Invalid or unexpected token" while node itself and the Linux gate pass.
*.mjs text eol=lf

# 2026-09-10: contract docs stay LF too, same class as the .sh/.mjs rules above —
# reviewed cross-platform (Windows authors, Linux CI) and a CRLF diff noise defeats review.
docs/contracts/*.md text eol=lf

# 2026-09-10: docs/ops/prod-ops-recipes.md is stored as an all-CRLF blob. Under
# core.autocrlf=true a plain `git add` renormalises it to LF, producing ~450 lines
# of diff churn around a handful of real edits on a file two lanes both edit --
# which makes it unreviewable and invites merge conflicts. Pinning it keeps the
# stored form stable so a diff shows only the actual change.
# NOT the reason: an earlier note in this repo claimed the churn split three
# `tr -d '"
'` commands across lines. That was wrong -- verified from raw blobs,
# the argument is a literal two-character backslash-r that EOL conversion never
# touches. Those commands are genuinely broken on main from some earlier edit and
# are repaired by item 01 slice s3, independently of this rule.
docs/ops/prod-ops-recipes.md text eol=crlf

# S0b fix round 1 (PR #738): scraper/config/field-manifest.json is committed LF in git's index, but a
# Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true reads it back CRLF, making the byte-level drift check in
# scripts/generate-field-manifest.mjs --check (and its CI wrapper) fail on Windows while passing on
# Linux CI. Pinning the working-copy line ending to LF on every machine removes the class, alongside
# the generator's own line-ending-normalized compare.
scraper/config/*.json text eol=lf

# 2026-09-20: the plan board sections are GENERATED (scripts/ops/build-plan-board.mjs).
# Two PRs that each corrected a DIFFERENT inventory row both rewrite this 90 KB file,
# and git interleaves them line-by-line into a conflict that is meaningless to resolve
# by hand. It cost PR #847 two rebases in one afternoon, and each DIRTY period ran ZERO
# CI workflows, so a real defect could have hidden there unseen.
#
# `-merge` makes git conflict the WHOLE file instead of its lines -- the honest signal:
# do not merge this, regenerate it. Proven 2026-09-20: two branches each touching the
# first <tr> conflict with 0 markers inside the file, vs interleaved hunks without it.
#
# Resolution is always the same two commands, and CI gates the result via
# `build-plan-board.mjs --check` in the detection-change job:
#   git checkout --theirs docs/design/board/plan-sections.generated.html
#   node scripts/ops/build-plan-board.mjs
docs/design/board/plan-sections.generated.html -merge

# index.html is a GENERATED build product that CI byte-compares
# (render-board.mjs --check), so its stored form should not vary by platform.
# NOTE: four eol=lf rules were added here for #850 and then removed -- an
# independent review proved line endings were never the cause. The gate was
# reporting REAL staleness against the PR merge ref, where main had moved
# plan-sections.generated.html. Only this rule is kept, on principle.
docs/design/board/index.html text eol=lf
# plan-sections.generated.html needs it for a different reason than index.html:
# `build-plan-board.mjs --check` byte-compares the committed file against a fresh
# generation. Without an explicit eol, git falls back to core.autocrlf and writes
# CRLF into a Windows worktree, so the gate fails on every Windows clone of main
# while passing on the Linux runner. Measured 2026-09-20 on a clean clone of
# facf3b30: identical 91582 bytes, zero CR in either, and git still reported the
# file modified. (The `-merge` rule above is separate — that one stops line-by-line
# conflict resolution; this one stops checkout renormalisation.)
docs/design/board/plan-sections.generated.html text eol=lf